         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=50mm]{figures/no_odd_even/fig9.pdf}}
         \caption{Case 1.2.2}
         \label{fig:c34}
\end{subfigure}

\caption{Case 1. Edge styles in Figures~\ref{fig:eightv_c3}--\ref{fig:eightv_c4}: red dashed, green solid, blue dotted, and brown dash-dotted.}
\label{fig:eightv_c3}
\end{figure}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Case 1.1} The blue perfect matching contains $(3,8)$. Then the blue perfect matching can be completed in two ways as shown in \cref{fig:c31} and \cref{fig:c32}. 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Case 1.1.1} (\cref{fig:c31}.) There is a blue-red alternating Hamiltonian cycle $16834752$ which is a union of two perfect matchings of different colours. By assumption such perfect matchings are not present in this case. Contradiction.
        \item \textbf{Case 1.1.2} (\cref{fig:c32}.) There is a blue-red alternating Hamiltonian cycle $17834652$ which is a union of two perfect matchings of different colours. By assumption such perfect matchings are not present in this case. Contradiction.
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Case 1.2} Vertex $3$ can have an edge with $6$ or $7$. Note that both are identical red neighbours and hence without loss of generality let the edge be with $7$. Then the blue perfect matching can be completed in two ways. 
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Case 1.2.1} (\cref{fig:c33}.) There is a blue-red alternating Hamiltonian cycle $16843752$. It is a union of two perfect matchings of different colours. Contradiction.
\item \textbf{Case 1.2.2} (\cref{fig:c34}.) There is a blue-red alternating Hamiltonian cycle $12564378$. It is a union of two perfect matchings of different colours. Contradiction.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Case 2.} We classify the remaining cases with respect to the fixed alternating
red-green Hamiltonian cycle. Label its vertices $1,\ldots,8$ in cyclic order.
For a monochromatic perfect matching $M$ of a colour
$i\notin\{\text{red},\text{green}\}$, call an edge of $M$ \emph{crossing}
if removing its endpoints from the Hamiltonian cycle leaves two paths of
even order. Say that $i$ is crossing if some such $M$ contains a crossing
edge. There are two cases.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  Some remaining non-redundant colour, say blue, is not crossing.  Then every edge of
every blue monochromatic perfect matching joins vertices of the same parity in
the cyclic ordering.
\item Every non-redundant colour other than red and green is crossing.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{figure}[t!]
    \centering
\centering    
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=\dimexpr\linewidth-2\fboxsep-2\fboxrule\relax]{figures/no_odd_even/fig3.pdf}}
         \caption{Case 2.1.1}
         \label{fig:c11}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=\dimexpr\linewidth-2\fboxsep-2\fboxrule\relax]{figures/no_odd_even/fig1.pdf}}
         \caption{Case 2.1.2 }
         \label{fig:c12}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=\dimexpr\linewidth-2\fboxsep-2\fboxrule\relax]{figures/no_odd_even/fig2.pdf}}
         \caption{Case 2.1.3}
         \label{fig:c13}
\end{subfigure}

\caption{Case 2.1}
\label{fig:eightv_c1}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Case 2.1.} Fix a blue monochromatic perfect matching $M_b$.
Since blue is not crossing, $M_b$ pairs vertices of equal parity: the four
odd vertices among themselves and the four even vertices among themselves.
Up to dihedral symmetry of the Hamiltonian cycle and interchange of red
and green, its three possible types are:
\[
\begin{aligned}
B_1&=\{(1,3),(2,8),(4,6),(5,7)\},\\
B_2&=\{(1,5),(2,4),(3,7),(6,8)\},\\
B_3&=\{(1,5),(2,6),(3,7),(4,8)\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
These are precisely the three cases shown in \Cref{fig:eightv_c1}.

\textbf{Case 2.1.1} (\cref{fig:c11}).  Consider the non-monochromatic vertex
colouring in which $1,2,3,8$ get colour blue and $4,5,6,7$ get colour green.
By \Cref{colour_split_lemma}, the weight of this colouring is
\[
w((1,3))w((2,8))w((4,5))w((6,7))\ne 0.
\]
This contradicts the GHZ condition, since every non-monochromatic inherited
vertex colouring must have weight $0$.

\textbf{Case 2.1.2} (\cref{fig:c12}). Consider the non-monochromatic vertex colouring in which $1,2$ get the colour red, $4,5$ get colour green and $3,6,7,8$ get the colour blue. From \cref{colour_split_lemma}, the weight of such a colouring is $w((1,2))w((4,5))w((3,7))w((6,8)) \neq 0$. But a non-monochromatic vertex colouring should have weight $0$. Contradiction.

We now record the structure forced by a crossing edge in a selected
monochromatic perfect matching $M_i$, for a colour $i$ other than red and
green. Up to a dihedral symmetry and interchange of red and green, let
$(8,3)$ be a crossing edge of $M_i$.
Consider the non-monochromatic vertex colouring assigning colour $i$ to
$3,8$, red to $1,2$, and green to $4,5,6,7$.
By \Cref{colour_split_lemma}, its weight is
$w((8,3))w((1,2))w(\mathbf{2}_{\{4,5,6,7\}})=0$.
The first two factors are non-zero, so
$w(\mathbf{2}_{\{4,5,6,7\}})=0$.
The green matching $(4,5),(6,7)$ has non-zero weight; its only possible
alternative is $(4,6),(5,7)$, since $(5,6)$ is red.
Thus $(4,6)$ and $(5,7)$ must both be green.
After $(8,3)$ is removed from $M_i$, four unmatched vertices lie in
$\{4,5,6,7\}$ and only two lie outside it. Therefore $M_i$ contains an
edge within $\{4,5,6,7\}$. The only available pair is $(4,7)$:
all other pairs already have red or green edges, and $G$ is simple.
The remaining vertices $1,2,5,6$ can be matched in exactly two ways,
$(1,5),(2,6)$ or $(1,6),(2,5)$, because $(1,2),(5,6)$ are red.
These are the two crossing-matching structures shown in
\Cref{fig:eightv_c2}; the derivation applies to any colour $i$, including brown.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
\centering    
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=\dimexpr\linewidth-2\fboxsep-2\fboxrule\relax]{figures/no_odd_even/fig4.pdf}}
         \caption{}
         \label{fig:c21}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=\dimexpr\linewidth-2\fboxsep-2\fboxrule\relax]{figures/no_odd_even/fig5.pdf}}
         \caption{ }
         \label{fig:c22}
\end{subfigure}

\caption{The two crossing-matching structures with three colours.}
\label{fig:eightv_c2}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Case 2.1.3.} This is the case shown in \cref{fig:c13}. Now consider the fourth colour, which is brown. 

\textbf{Case 2.1.3.1} If brown is not crossing, no brown monochromatic perfect matching contains a crossing edge. The union of any selected brown monochromatic perfect matching with the fixed red-green Hamiltonian cycle must then be one of the three structures from \cref{fig:eightv_c1}. Further, as all antipodal pairs have a blue edge between them, the union of a brown perfect matching with a Hamiltonian cycle with alternating red and green colours must be like that of \cref{fig:c11}, which was already proven to be not possible.

\textbf{Case 2.1.3.2} If brown is crossing, select a brown monochromatic perfect matching containing a crossing edge. By the crossing-edge derivation above, its union with the fixed red-green Hamiltonian cycle must be one of the two structures from \cref{fig:eightv_c2}. Further, as all antipodal pairs have a blue edge between them, the union of a brown perfect matching with a Hamiltonian cycle with alternating red and green colours must be like that of \cref{fig:c21}. Now consider the non-monochromatic vertex colouring in which $1,2,5,6$ get the colour brown, and $3,4,7,8$ get the colour blue. From \cref{colour_split_lemma} and \cref{partition_split_lemma}, we know that the weight of such a vertex colouring is $w((1,6))w((2,5))w((3,7))w((4,8))\ne 0.$ Contradiction.


\textbf{Case 2.2.} In this case, every non-redundant colour other than red and green is crossing.
Select a blue monochromatic perfect matching and a brown monochromatic
perfect matching, each containing a crossing edge.
The crossing-edge derivation above applies separately to both selected
matchings, so each has one of the two structures in \Cref{fig:eightv_c2},
up to a symmetry of the fixed red-green Hamiltonian cycle.
Their combined configurations are shown in \Cref{fig:eightv_c4}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
\centering    
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=\dimexpr\linewidth-2\fboxsep-2\fboxrule\relax]{figures/no_odd_even/fig10.pdf}}
         \caption{Case 2.2.1}
         \label{fig:c41}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=35mm]{figures/no_odd_even/fig11.pdf}}
         \caption{Case 2.2.2 }
         \label{fig:c42}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.3\textwidth}
         \centering
         \fbox{\includegraphics[width=\dimexpr\linewidth-2\fboxsep-2\fboxrule\relax]{figures/no_odd_even/fig12.pdf}}
         \caption{Case 2.2.3}
         \label{fig:c43}
\end{subfigure}

\caption{Case 2.2 with $4$ colours}
\label{fig:eightv_c4}
\end{figure}

The selected blue and brown matchings are edge-disjoint because the graph
is simple. Up to the symmetries of the
alternating Hamiltonian cycle and interchange of the names blue and brown, the
possible pairs of blue and brown matchings are exactly the three configurations
shown in \cref{fig:eightv_c4}.

\textbf{Case 2.2.1 and Case 2.2.2} (\cref{fig:c41} and \cref{fig:c42}) {In both configurations, the following argument applies.  Consider the non-monochromatic vertex colouring in which the vertices $5,6,7,8$ are red, $1,4$ are brown, and $2,3$ are green. From \cref{colour_split_lemma}, this is possible only if the edges $(5,7)$ and $(6,8)$ are red. Now consider the non-monochromatic vertex colouring in which vertices $4,5,6,7$ are green, vertices $1,2$ are red, and vertices $8,3$ are blue. From \cref{colour_split_lemma}, this is possible only if the edges $(5,7)$ and $(6,4)$ are green. But $(5,7)$ is already red. Contradiction.}

\textbf{Case 2.2.3} (\cref{fig:c43}) Consider the vertex colouring in which $1,2,5,6$ get the colour red and $3,4,7,8$ get the colour blue. From \cref{colour_split_lemma} and \cref{partition_split_lemma}, the weight of the vertex colouring is $w((1,2))w((5,6))w((3,7))w((4,8))\neq 0$. Contradiction.

Therefore, $\mu(G) \leq 3$.
